\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a finite planar point set, a $k$-set is a subset of $k$ points separable from the others by a line. Determine matching upper and lower bounds for the maximum number of $k$-sets, and for the number of halving lines that split the remaining points equally.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Geometry}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For a finite planar point set, a $k$-set is a subset of $k$ points separable from the others by a line. Determine matching upper and lower bounds for the maximum number of $k$-sets, and for the number of halving lines that split the remaining points equally.

